Push an existing HTML project to GitHub using VS Code
Step 1: Open your project in VS Code
Suppose your folder structure is:
MyWebsite/
   index.html
   style.css
   script.js
1.	Open VS Code.
2.	Click File → Open Folder.
3.	Select MyWebsite.
4.	Click Terminal → New Terminal.
Your terminal should show something like:
/home/aritrika/MyWebsite$
Step 2: Initialize Git
git init
Output:
Initialized empty Git repository in
/home/aritrika/MyWebsite/.git/
This converts your project folder into a Git repository.
Step 3: Configure your username and email
(Only required the first time.)
git config --global user.name "Aritrika Das"

git config --global user.email "your-email@gmail.com"
Verify:
git config --list
Step 4: Check repository status
git status
Output:
Untracked files:
    index.html
    style.css
    script.js
Git has detected your files but isn't tracking them yet.
Step 5: Add files to staging area
git add .
The . means add all files in the current project folder.
Check:
git status
Output:
Changes to be committed:

    new file: index.html
    new file: style.css
    new file: script.js
Step 6: Commit changes
git commit -m "Initial commit"
Output:
[master abc1234] Initial commit
3 files changed
Your files are now saved in your local Git repository.
Step 7: Create a GitHub repository
1.	Open GitHub.
2.	Log in.
3.	Click the + icon → New repository.
4.	Enter repository name: MyWebsite.
5.	Select Public.
6.	Click Create repository.
Copy the repository URL. It'll look like:
https://github.com/username/MyWebsite.git
Don't initialize it with a README, license or gitignore because you already have a local project.
Step 8: Connect local project to GitHub
Back in VS Code terminal:
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/MyWebsite.git
Replace username with your actual GitHub username.
Check connection:
git remote -v
Output:
origin  https://github.com/username/MyWebsite.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/username/MyWebsite.git (push)
Step 9: Push to GitHub
First, rename your branch to main:
git branch -M main
Now push:
git push -u origin main
Step 10: Verify on GitHub
Refresh your GitHub repository page.
You should see all your files there!
