PROGRAM 5: CI/CD using Jenkins
CI: Continuous Integration — automatically builds and tests code changes.
CD: Continuous Delivery/Deployment — prepares or deploys tested applications.
Pipeline: Sequence of automated Jenkins stages.
Stage: Individual step in a pipeline.
Jenkinsfile: File containing pipeline instructions.

Steps:
Open http://localhost:8080.
Click New Item.
Enter MyPipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
Scroll to Pipeline.
Select Pipeline script.
Paste:
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                echo 'Getting source code'
            }
        }
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building application'
            }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                echo 'Testing application'
            }
        }
        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                echo 'Deploying application'
            }
        }
    }
}
Click Save.
Click Build Now.
Open build number → Console Output.
Output: Jenkins browser displays all stages and Finished: SUCCESS.
Note: This is a demonstration pipeline; echo commands do not actually build or deploy an application.







Docker commands
1. Check whether Docker is working
     docker --version
     docker info
2. Image management
     docker pull ubuntu
     docker images
     docker rmi IMAGE_ID
3. Container lifecycle
     docker run -it ubuntu bash
You'll enter a new Linux shell inside the container.
Inside that container, try:
    pwd
    ls
    exit
Now run these commands in your normal Linux terminal:
    docker ps
    docker ps -a
    docker stop CONTAINER_ID
    docker start CONTAINER_ID
    docker rm CONTAINER_ID
4. Working with content inside containers
Use a running container for these commands.
     docker exec -it CONTAINER_ID bash
This opens a shell inside an already-running container.
For copying files, first make sure the host file exists:
      touch file.txt
Copy it into the container:
      docker cp file.txt CONTAINER_ID:/tmp/
To copy a file back from the container:
      docker cp CONTAINER_ID:/tmp/file.txt .
For the second command, /tmp/file.txt must already exist inside the container. You can create it using:
      docker exec CONTAINER_ID bash -c "echo Hello > /tmp/file.txt"
5. Logs and inspection
      docker logs CONTAINER_ID
      docker inspect CONTAINER_ID
6. Volumes
      docker volume create mydata
      docker volume ls
      docker volume inspect mydata
      docker run -it -v mydata:/app/data ubuntu bash
Inside the container, run:
      echo "Hello Docker" > /app/data/test.txt
      exit
The volume stores data persistently, even after the container is removed.


Docker containerized
1. Create a folder
      mkdir docker-html-program
      cd docker-html-program
This creates a folder and moves into it.
2. Create index.html
      nano index.html
Write this HTML code:
      <!DOCTYPE html>
      <html>
      <head>
          <title>Hello Docker</title>
      </head>
      <body>
          <h1>Hello Docker Demonstration!</h1>
          <p>This is My first Docker containerized web application.</p>
      </body>
      </html>
Save and exit:
•	Ctrl + O → Enter
•	Ctrl + X
3. Create Dockerfile
      nano Dockerfile
Write:
      FROM nginx
      COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/
Save and exit.
What do these lines mean?
•	FROM nginx – Uses the Nginx image as the base image.
•	COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/ – Copies your HTML file into Nginx's web server directory.
4. Build the Docker image
      docker build -t myhtml .
•	docker build – Builds an image.
•	-t myhtml – Names the image myhtml.
•	. – Uses the current directory as the build context.
5. Run the container
Your record says:
      docker run -d -p 8080:80 m
